**Is it recurring?** Special dividends of this magnitude are episodic rather than annual, but they do recur roughly every 2–3 years as a pattern (Costco paid $10/share in 2020, $7/share in 2017, $5/share in 2015, and $7/share in 2012 before the $15/share payout in January 2024). So while the FY2024 equity dip itself was a **one-off event tied to that specific special dividend**, the *practice* of periodic special dividends is a recurring feature of Costco's capital-return policy — meaning investors should expect similar equity "step-downs" to recur in future years whenever the next special dividend is declared, rather than viewing FY2024 as an anomaly never to repeat.

**Effect on ROE:** Because the special dividend shrank the equity denominator (average/ending equity) while net income continued rising, the reported 27.8% ROE for FY2024 is inflated relative to a scenario without the special dividend — it reflects a smaller equity base rather than a structural improvement in returns on the underlying business. Analysts should treat the ROE jump as partly mechanical (denominator effect from the special dividend) rather than purely fundamental margin/profitability improvement.

